{
 "service": "exterior-detailing",
 "city": "lannon-wi",
 "title": "Exterior Detailing & Decontamination in Lannon, WI | Sudz Up Detailing",
 "meta": "Exterior detailing for Lannon, WI. Limestone quarry dust bonded to paint removed by chemical and clay decontamination, not washing. About 25 minutes. Call 414-286-1609.",
 "h1": "Exterior Detailing in Lannon, Wisconsin",
 "lede": "Lannon vehicles are our clearest demonstration of the difference between washing and decontamination, because the thing on the paint is alkaline stone.",
 "glance": [("From our shop", "About 25 minutes"), ("Price", "Quoted"), ("Key stage", "Clay decon"), ("Local factor", "Quarry dust")],
 "body": [
   ("Why quarry dust does not rinse",
    ["The quarries around Lannon produce dolomitic limestone, and the fine dust they release is calcium-magnesium carbonate \u2014 alkaline, extremely fine, and mineral rather than organic.",
     "Mineral dust behaves differently from road grime on paint. When it lands dry it sits loose and a rinse moves it. But when it lands on dew or is rained on and then dries, the minerals recrystallise on the surface and bond to the clear coat. At that point water does not touch it, and neither does soap, because there is nothing water-soluble left to dissolve.",
     "This is the chalky cast Lannon owners describe on the bonnet and roof that survives every wash. It is not dirt. It is a thin mineral layer bonded to the paint, and the only thing that removes it is mechanical decontamination."]),
   ("The clay stage does the work here",
    ["On most vehicles the chemical iron remover is the dramatic stage and clay is the finishing touch. On a Lannon vehicle that reverses.",
     "Bonded mineral film is exactly what clay is for. Lubricated and drawn across the panel, it shears the recrystallised layer off level with the clear coat, and on a car that has never had it done the amount that comes off is startling \u2014 the clay goes grey within a panel or two and has to be folded repeatedly. It is slow, it is by hand, and it is the reason a decontaminated Lannon vehicle feels like glass afterwards.",
     "Iron removal still matters \u2014 haul truck brake dust is real \u2014 but it is the secondary stage here rather than the primary one."]),
   ("Protection is not optional afterwards",
    ["Decontaminating a Lannon vehicle and leaving the paint bare is a job that lasts until the next dew cycle in a dust cloud.",
     "Protection is what makes the work hold. Sealant or, better, a <a href=\"/services/ceramic-coating/\">ceramic coating</a> gives the paint a surface that mineral dust does not readily bond to, so the next rinse actually removes it instead of the film rebuilding within weeks.",
     "On a vehicle that lives in the village, we would not do the decontamination without the protection. It is the difference between a result and an afternoon."]),
 ],
 "faq": [
   ("Why does the chalky film on my paint survive every wash?",
    "Because it is bonded mineral, not dirt. Limestone dust that dries on damp paint recrystallises onto the clear coat, and nothing water-soluble remains for a wash to dissolve. Clay decontamination shears it off."),
   ("Is the clay stage really necessary?",
    "On a Lannon vehicle it is the main event. Chemical iron removal handles brake dust; only mechanical clay removes bonded mineral film."),
   ("Will it just come back?",
    "If the paint is left bare, yes, within weeks. Sealant or a coating gives the surface something mineral dust does not readily bond to, so the next rinse actually removes it."),
 ],
},
