{
 "service": "odor-removal",
 "city": "waukesha-wi",
 "title": "Car Odor Removal in Waukesha, WI \u2014 Water Ingress & River-Corridor Damp | Sudz Up",
 "meta": "Car odor removal for Waukesha, WI. Water that got into the cabin from a flooded lot or a leak, river-corridor damp, and the musty smell that follows. Assessed per vehicle. Call 414-286-1609.",
 "h1": "Odor Removal in Waukesha, Wisconsin",
 "lede": "The most common Waukesha odor job starts with water that got into the car and a carpet that felt dry a week later.",
 "glance": [("From our shop", "About 35 minutes"), ("Price", "Quoted"), ("Common source", "Water ingress"), ("First step", "Find the water")],
 "body": [
   ("The carpet that feels dry",
    ["Water that reaches a cabin \u2014 from a lot along the Fox River that flooded briefly, from a blocked sunroof drain, from a failed door seal \u2014 goes through the carpet and into the padding beneath it within minutes. The carpet surface then dries in a day or two. The padding does not.",
     "Padding is foam. It holds water like a sponge, it is sealed under the carpet and the seats, and it has no airflow. It can stay wet for months. The owner checks the carpet, finds it dry, assumes the problem is over, and three weeks later the car smells musty and they cannot find why.",
     "That smell is mold establishing in the padding. It is the single most common odor job we see from the river corridor, and it is one where the timing matters more than most: the sooner the padding is extracted, the less has grown."]),
   ("Finding where it came in",
    ["We do not treat a water smell without finding the water, because if the source is still active the smell comes back after the next rain and the money was spent twice.",
     "On a vehicle that was in a flooded lot, the source is known and the job is drying and extraction. On a vehicle where nobody knows how the water got in, the usual suspects are sunroof drains blocked with debris, cowl drains under the windscreen blocked with leaves, door membranes that have come unstuck, and windscreen seals. We check those first and we will tell you where we think the water is coming from before quoting any cleaning.",
     "Some of those are repair jobs rather than detailing, and we will say so. A torn door membrane needs fixing, not extracting around."]),
   ("What extraction does and does not fix",
    ["Recent water, padding damp but mold not yet established: extraction pulls the moisture out and the vehicle is fine. This is the case where acting inside a couple of weeks makes all the difference.",
     "Older water, mold established: extraction removes the growth and the moisture and the great majority of the smell. It may take two passes.",
     "Long-standing saturation, months of it: the honest answer is sometimes that the padding or a section of carpet needs replacing rather than cleaning. Foam that has been wet for a season does not always recover. We will tell you which case you have after lifting the carpet, not before.",
     "For the general mechanism, our <a href=\"/guides/mold-in-a-car-after-a-leak/\">guide to mold after a leak</a> covers where water gets in and how to check for it yourself."]),
 ],
 "faq": [
   ("My car was in a flooded lot. The carpet is dry. Is it fine?",
    "Probably not. The carpet dries in a day; the padding underneath holds water for months. Lift the carpet and check. If the padding is damp, it needs extraction before mold establishes, and the sooner the better."),
   ("Why does the musty smell come back after cleaning?",
    "Either the padding was not extracted \u2014 surface cleaning does not reach it \u2014 or water is still getting in. We find the source before treating the smell for exactly that reason."),
   ("How far is Waukesha from your shop?",
    "About thirty-five minutes to 2948 WI-83 in Hartford. Odor work is assessed before quoting; call or text 414-286-1609 to arrange a look."),
 ],
},
