   ("Hay goes where a vacuum cannot",
    ["Hay is light, it is dry, and it travels on air. It goes into seat rails, into the gap between the seat base and the console, into the door pocket felt, into the seat belt retractor slots, and behind the pedals. It gets into places a vacuum nozzle cannot reach and it does not move when the nozzle passes over the opening, because a vacuum needs airflow and there is none inside a seat rail.",
     "Compressed air first is the whole approach. Blow the cabin out \u2014 every seam, rail, slot and gap \u2014 so the hay lifts into the open, then collect it. On a horse vehicle that step takes longer than on any other vehicle type and it does most of the work."]),
   ("Arena footing",
    ["Riding arena footing is engineered \u2014 a mix of fine sand, fibre and sometimes rubber crumb \u2014 and it is finer than beach sand by a wide margin. It comes in on boots and works deep into carpet pile, and because it is fine it goes further down than sand would and packs harder.",
     "It needs mechanical agitation to break it out of the pile and then extraction. A vacuum passes over packed footing without lifting it, which is why horse vehicles so often arrive having been vacuumed weekly and still gritty underfoot."]),
